/* WISEBYTE LESSONS · Ancient & Medieval batch Y */
WB.L("pompeii-herculaneum", "ancient", "beginner", "Pompeii and Herculaneum: towns frozen in time", "In AD 79 a volcano buried two Roman towns in a matter of hours. What it destroyed, it also preserved: bread in the oven, graffiti on the walls and the last moments of the people who lived there.",
  [
    ["A mountain nobody feared", "The people of Pompeii did not know Vesuvius was a volcano.", "Picture a busy market town on the Bay of Naples, with vineyards climbing the slopes of a green mountain behind it. To the Romans, Vesuvius was simply good farming land. It had not erupted in living memory, and there was no word in Latin for a volcano as a type of mountain. Pompeii had perhaps 10,000 to 12,000 residents, while nearby Herculaneum was a smaller, wealthier seaside town. In AD 62 a strong earthquake shook the region, and when Vesuvius finally erupted, some buildings were still being repaired. Nobody connected the tremors with what was coming. That ignorance is a big part of why the eruption proved so deadly, and why so much was left behind exactly where it stood."],
    ["The day the sky fell", "The eruption came in two deadly stages over about a day.", "Around midday, Vesuvius blasted a towering column of ash and pumice high into the sky. Pliny the Younger, watching from across the bay, later compared its shape to an umbrella pine tree. For hours, light pumice stones rained onto Pompeii, piling up on roofs until many collapsed. Some residents fled; others sheltered and waited. Then, overnight and into the next morning, the column collapsed and sent pyroclastic surges racing down the mountain. These were avalanches of superheated gas and ash moving faster than anyone could run, and they killed almost everyone still in their path. Herculaneum, closer and downwind in a different way, was struck by these surges and then buried under many metres of volcanic material that later hardened into rock."],
    ["Pliny's eyewitness account", "A teenager's letters give us the only surviving eyewitness record.", "Pliny the Younger was about 17 when he watched the eruption from Misenum, across the bay. His uncle, Pliny the Elder, was a famous naturalist and commander of the Roman fleet stationed there. The elder Pliny launched ships to investigate and to rescue friends living along the coast, but he died at Stabiae, probably overcome by fumes or heart failure. Years later, the historian Tacitus asked the nephew what had happened, and Pliny wrote two vivid letters in reply that still survive. They describe darkness 'like a sealed room', shaking ground and panicked crowds. Volcanologists still call this explosive type of eruption 'Plinian' in his honour. The traditional date is 24 August, but clues such as autumn fruit and a charcoal inscription suggest it may have happened in October."],
    ["Bodies in the ash", "Plaster casts reveal the final postures of the victims.", "As the ash around Pompeii's victims hardened, their bodies slowly decayed, leaving hollow spaces shaped exactly like the people who had been there. In 1863, the archaeologist Giuseppe Fiorelli had the idea of pouring liquid plaster into these cavities. When the plaster set and the ash was chipped away, the casts showed people curled up, shielding their faces, or holding one another. Even a dog straining at its chain was preserved. At Herculaneum, the story was different. For centuries experts assumed most people had escaped, until the 1980s, when hundreds of skeletons were found crowded into boathouses by the old shoreline. They had been waiting for rescue from the sea. Their bones now give scientists rich information about Roman health, diet and injuries."],
    ["Graffiti, takeaway and election posters", "Pompeii shows ordinary Roman life in remarkable detail.", "Walk down a Pompeii street today and you pass stepping stones for crossing roads, ruts worn by cart wheels and dozens of street-food counters called thermopolia, with big jars set into stone benches. Many Romans living in small apartments did not cook at home, so grabbing a hot meal was normal. The walls are covered in painted election notices urging people to vote for local candidates, and in thousands of scratched graffiti messages: jokes, insults, love notes, boasts and accounts of debts. Bakeries still contain millstones and ovens, and archaeologists have found carbonised loaves of bread. Grand homes like the House of the Vettii hold colourful frescoes, and one doorway mosaic shows a fierce dog with the words 'Cave canem', meaning 'beware of the dog'."],
    ["Rediscovery and reading the unreadable", "Modern technology is unlocking scrolls that were burned into lumps.", "After the eruption the towns were largely forgotten, buried beneath new soil and later villages. Workers digging tunnels and wells stumbled on ruins, and formal excavations began at Herculaneum in 1738 and at Pompeii in 1748. Early digging was often more treasure hunt than science, but methods gradually improved. One of the most exciting finds was a luxurious villa at Herculaneum containing a library of papyrus scrolls, turned into fragile black lumps by the heat. For centuries, attempts to unroll them destroyed many. Recently, researchers have scanned scrolls with powerful X-rays and used machine learning to spot faint traces of ink inside them. In 2023 and 2024, teams revealed whole passages of Greek philosophy that no one had read for nearly 2,000 years."],
    ["Why Pompeii still matters", "The site is a lesson in history, science and living with risk.", "Most of what we know about the ancient world comes from writers who described emperors, wars and great events. Pompeii and Herculaneum show instead what happened on an ordinary day: what people ate, how they decorated their homes, what made them laugh. Excavation continues, and new frescoes and rooms are still being uncovered. The sites face modern threats too, including weather, crowds of tourists and the challenge of protecting walls that were never meant to stand open to the sky. Meanwhile, Vesuvius remains an active volcano, and hundreds of thousands of people live in the zones around it. Italian authorities maintain evacuation plans and constant monitoring. The ruins are a reminder that knowing the warning signs can be the difference between disaster and survival."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What happened in the Pompeii region in AD 62?", ["A major flood", "A strong earthquake", "A small eruption of Vesuvius", "A plague outbreak"], 1, "A powerful earthquake damaged the area, and some repairs were still unfinished in AD 79."],
    [1, "What killed most people who stayed in Pompeii?", ["Lava flows", "A tsunami", "Pyroclastic surges of hot gas and ash", "Poisoned water"], 2, "Fast-moving pyroclastic surges of superheated gas and ash swept through the towns."],
    [2, "Why are explosive eruptions like this called 'Plinian'?", ["After a Roman god", "After the town of Plinia", "After Pliny the Younger, who described it", "After a modern volcanologist"], 2, "The term honours Pliny the Younger, whose letters describe the eruption."],
    [3, "Where were hundreds of skeletons found at Herculaneum?", ["In boathouses by the old shoreline", "In the main temple", "In the city baths", "Inside the theatre"], 0, "People crowded into boathouses by the shore, apparently hoping to be rescued by sea."],
    [5, "How are researchers reading the burned Herculaneum scrolls?", ["By soaking them in water", "By carefully peeling them by hand", "By scanning them with X-rays and using machine learning", "By testing them with chemical dyes"], 2, "X-ray scans combined with machine learning reveal ink inside the rolled-up scrolls."]
  ]);

WB.L("mongol-empire", "ancient", "beginner", "Genghis Khan and the Mongol Empire", "A boy abandoned on the harsh steppe grew up to build the largest connected land empire in history. Here is how the Mongols conquered so much, so quickly, and what their rule left behind.",
  [
    ["An outcast on the steppe", "Genghis Khan began life as a poor and hunted outsider.", "The boy who became Genghis Khan was born Temüjin, probably around 1162, on the grasslands of what is now Mongolia. When he was still a child, his father was poisoned by a rival tribe, and his own clan abandoned the family. Temüjin, his mother and his siblings survived by gathering roots and fishing, at the very bottom of steppe society. As a young man he was captured and held prisoner, but he escaped. These hard years shaped him. He learned that loyalty mattered more than birth, and he slowly gathered followers by rewarding those who stood by him. By 1206, after years of wars and alliances, he had united the Mongol and Turkic tribes, and a great assembly proclaimed him Genghis Khan."],
    ["A new kind of army", "Discipline and organisation made the Mongols unstoppable.", "Mongol boys learned to ride almost as soon as they could walk, and every man was a skilled horse archer. Genghis Khan turned these riders into a superbly organised army. Troops were grouped in units of ten, a hundred, a thousand and ten thousand, and he deliberately mixed men from different tribes so that old loyalties gave way to loyalty to the khan. Each warrior brought several horses, so he could switch to a fresh one and cover huge distances quickly. The Mongols were masters of feigned retreat, pretending to flee before turning on pursuers. They also learned fast, recruiting Chinese and Persian engineers to build siege engines that could break city walls a nomadic army could never otherwise have taken."],
    ["Conquest and terror", "The Mongol conquests caused enormous death and destruction.", "It would be wrong to romanticise the Mongols. Cities that surrendered were often spared, but those that resisted could be destroyed and their populations killed, and the Mongols spread stories of these massacres to frighten others into giving up. After the ruler of the Khwarazmian Empire in Central Asia killed Mongol envoys, Genghis Khan's armies devastated the region's great cities in the years after 1219. Decades later, in 1258, his grandson Hulegu captured Baghdad, then one of the world's great centres of learning, and much of it was sacked. Historians debate the numbers, which ancient writers may have exaggerated, but there is no doubt that millions died across Asia and Eastern Europe during the conquests, and some regions took centuries to recover."],
    ["The largest land empire", "At its height the empire stretched from Korea to Eastern Europe.", "Genghis Khan died in 1227, but his sons and grandsons kept expanding. Under his son Ögedei, Mongol armies swept into Russia, Poland and Hungary. In 1241 they suddenly withdrew from Europe, and many historians link this to news of Ögedei's death, which required the princes to return home to choose a new leader. At its peak in the later 1200s, the Mongol realm covered a vast belt of Asia and Eastern Europe, making it the largest connected land empire ever. It soon split into four large states: the Golden Horde in Russia, the Ilkhanate in Persia, the Chagatai Khanate in Central Asia, and the Yuan dynasty in China, which was founded by Genghis's grandson Kublai Khan."],
    ["Horses, passports and post", "The Mongols ran their empire with a brilliant relay system.", "Ruling such a huge area needed fast communication. The Mongols built a network of relay stations called the yam, spaced across the empire, where official riders could swap horses, get food and rest. Messages could travel remarkable distances in days rather than weeks. Important travellers carried a paiza, a tablet of metal or wood that worked a little like a passport, showing they were under the khan's protection and entitled to help along the way. The Mongols also took censuses, collected taxes and wrote laws. Genghis Khan had his people adopt a script borrowed from the Uyghurs so that Mongolian could be written down. For a nomadic people with no tradition of cities, this was a remarkable feat of administration."],
    ["The Mongol Peace", "Mongol rule made long-distance trade across Asia safer for a time.", "Once the fighting died down in many regions, the empire's size had an unexpected effect. Because one family controlled the routes from China to the Middle East and Russia, merchants could travel the Silk Roads with more safety than before. Historians sometimes call this period the Pax Mongolica, or Mongol Peace. Goods, ideas and people moved across Eurasia: paper money, gunpowder and printing techniques travelled west, while Persian astronomers and doctors worked in China. The Mongols were generally tolerant of different religions, and Buddhists, Muslims, Christians and Daoists could be found at the khan's court. The darker side of this connectedness is that diseases could travel too, and many historians think the Black Death spread along these same routes."],
    ["Legacy and limits", "The empire faded, but it reshaped the map of Eurasia.", "The Mongols were not invincible. In 1260 the Mamluks of Egypt defeated them at Ain Jalut in Palestine, halting their advance. Kublai Khan's invasions of Japan in 1274 and 1281 both failed, partly because storms wrecked his fleets; the Japanese later called such winds kamikaze, or 'divine wind'. Over the following century, the separate khanates weakened, and in 1368 the Yuan dynasty was driven out of China by the Ming. Yet the Mongols left deep marks. They helped shape modern Russia, linked East and West more closely than ever before, and spread technologies that changed the world. In Mongolia today, Genghis Khan is honoured as a national founder, his face on banknotes and a giant statue near the capital."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What was Genghis Khan's birth name?", ["Kublai", "Temüjin", "Ögedei", "Hulegu"], 1, "He was born Temüjin and was proclaimed Genghis Khan in 1206."],
    [1, "Why did Genghis Khan mix men from different tribes in his army units?", ["To teach them new languages", "To replace tribal loyalty with loyalty to the khan", "Because some tribes had no horses", "To make units smaller"], 1, "Mixing tribes broke down old rivalries and bound soldiers to the khan."],
    [4, "What was the Mongol 'yam'?", ["A type of bow", "A tax on grain", "A network of relay stations for riders", "A religious festival"], 2, "The yam was a relay system that let messengers swap horses and travel fast."],
    [5, "What does 'Pax Mongolica' refer to?", ["A peace treaty with Japan", "A period when Mongol rule made trade routes safer", "The Mongol law code", "A Mongol capital city"], 1, "It describes the era when unified Mongol control made Eurasian trade easier."],
    [6, "Who defeated the Mongols at Ain Jalut in 1260?", ["The Ming dynasty", "The Japanese", "The Mamluks of Egypt", "The Byzantines"], 2, "The Mamluks stopped the Mongol advance into Egypt at Ain Jalut."]
  ]);

WB.L("mali-empire-mansa-musa", "ancient", "beginner", "Mansa Musa and the golden empire of Mali", "In 1324 a West African king travelled to Mecca with so much gold that he reportedly upset the economy of Cairo. His empire of Mali was one of the richest in the medieval world.",
  [
    ["A king who dazzled Cairo", "Mansa Musa's pilgrimage made Mali famous far beyond Africa.", "In 1324, a vast caravan arrived in Cairo on its way to Mecca. At its head was Mansa Musa, ruler of the Mali Empire, travelling with thousands of attendants and camels reportedly loaded with gold. The Arab writer al-Umari, who spoke to people in Cairo who had met the king, reported that Musa gave away and spent so much gold that its value in the city fell and had still not recovered years later. Exact figures from the time are impossible to check, and later retellings have grown with each telling. But the story spread across the Mediterranean world, and it put Mali firmly on the mental map of merchants and rulers who had barely heard of West Africa before."],
    ["The lion king of Mali", "Mali was founded by a legendary hero named Sundiata Keita.", "The Mali Empire grew out of a small Mande kingdom along the upper Niger River in West Africa. Its founder, Sundiata Keita, is remembered in an epic that is still performed today. In the story, Sundiata is born unable to walk, mocked as a child and driven into exile, but he returns as a great warrior and defeats a powerful sorcerer king named Sumanguru in a decisive battle, usually dated to around the 1230s. Historians treat the epic as a mix of history and legend, but they agree Sundiata built a strong new state. His successors expanded it until Mali stretched across much of West Africa, from the Atlantic coast inland to the trading cities of the Niger bend."],
    ["Gold and salt", "Mali grew rich by controlling the trade in gold and salt.", "For centuries, much of the gold used in the Mediterranean world and the Middle East came from West Africa. Rich goldfields lay in regions such as Bambuk and Bure, within or near Mali's lands. Mali did not need to mine all of it directly: it controlled and taxed the trade. In the other direction came salt, dug in slabs from desert mines such as Taghaza in the Sahara. In hot climates people need salt to survive, so it was extremely valuable, at times traded weight for weight against gold, according to some accounts. Camel caravans carried goods across the desert in journeys that could take months, and cities on the edge of the Sahara grew wealthy as meeting points between desert and river trade."],
    ["Timbuktu, city of books", "Under Mali, Timbuktu became a famous centre of Islamic learning.", "Today 'Timbuktu' is used in English to mean the remotest place imaginable, but in the 1300s it was a thriving crossroads. After returning from Mecca, Mansa Musa is said to have brought scholars and an architect back with him, and the Djinguereyber Mosque in Timbuktu, built of mud brick and wood, is traditionally linked to his reign. The city became a centre where scholars taught law, astronomy, mathematics and medicine, and families collected manuscripts over generations. Many of these handwritten books survived in private libraries for centuries. When armed groups occupied Timbuktu in 2012, librarians and residents quietly smuggled hundreds of thousands of manuscripts out of the city in boxes and sacks to keep them safe."],
    ["How the empire worked", "Mali was a large, organised state with strong traditions.", "At its height, Mali was a patchwork of provinces and tributary kingdoms ruled by a mansa, meaning king or emperor. The mansa's court was the centre of power, and local rulers kept their positions as long as they stayed loyal and paid tribute. Islam was the faith of the court and of many merchants, but many ordinary people kept older local beliefs, and rulers often balanced the two. Griots, professional storytellers and musicians, were crucial. They memorised royal genealogies and history and passed them on through song and speech, which is how the Sundiata epic survived. Farming along the Niger River fed the population, with crops such as millet, sorghum and rice, while the river itself served as a highway for boats and trade."],
    ["A traveller's report", "Ibn Battuta left a vivid description of Mali in the 1350s.", "The great Moroccan traveller Ibn Battuta, who journeyed across much of the medieval Muslim world, visited Mali around 1352 to 1353, a few years after Mansa Musa's death. He described the court of Musa's brother, Mansa Sulayman, with its ceremonies, interpreters and royal guards. He grumbled that the king's gift to him was disappointingly small, but he also praised the safety of the roads, saying travellers had little reason to fear robbers. He noted how seriously people took Friday prayers and how keen they were for their children to learn the Quran. His account is one of the few eyewitness descriptions of Mali from outside, and it gives a rare glimpse of everyday life in the empire."],
    ["Decline and legacy", "Mali faded, but its fame lives on in maps, music and memory.", "After Mansa Musa, Mali faced disputes over succession, rebellions by provinces and attacks from desert peoples. In the 1400s, the rising Songhay Empire, based further east along the Niger at Gao, took over Timbuktu and much of the trade. Mali shrank to a smaller kingdom. Yet its legacy endured. The Catalan Atlas, a famous map made in Spain around 1375, shows a crowned African ruler holding a gold nugget, a sign of how Europeans pictured West Africa. The modern country of Mali, which gained independence from France in 1960, took its name from the old empire. And griots still sing the epic of Sundiata, keeping alive a history that was never only written down."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What did al-Umari report about Mansa Musa's visit to Cairo?", ["He was refused entry", "He spent so much gold that its value fell", "He built a famous mosque there", "He married the sultan's daughter"], 1, "Al-Umari wrote that so much gold flooded Cairo that its value dropped for years."],
    [1, "Who founded the Mali Empire according to its famous epic?", ["Sundiata Keita", "Mansa Sulayman", "Sumanguru", "Ibn Battuta"], 0, "Sundiata Keita is remembered as Mali's founder in the epic still performed by griots."],
    [2, "What valuable good came south across the Sahara to trade for gold?", ["Silk", "Salt", "Ivory", "Pepper"], 1, "Salt from desert mines such as Taghaza was traded for West African gold."],
    [4, "What role did griots play in Mali?", ["Tax collectors", "Royal bodyguards", "Storytellers who preserved history in song and speech", "Caravan leaders"], 2, "Griots memorised and performed history and genealogies, preserving them over generations."],
    [6, "Which empire took over Timbuktu and much of Mali's trade in the 1400s?", ["The Ottoman Empire", "The Ghana Empire", "The Songhay Empire", "The Almoravid Empire"], 2, "The Songhay Empire, based at Gao, rose to replace Mali as the region's great power."]
  ]);

WB.L("ancient-olympics", "ancient", "beginner", "The original Olympic Games", "For more than a thousand years, Greeks gathered at Olympia to run, wrestle and race chariots in honour of Zeus. The ancient Games were holier, rougher and stranger than the modern version.",
  [
    ["A festival for Zeus", "The Olympics began as a religious festival, not just a sporting event.", "Imagine tens of thousands of people camped in the summer heat around a sacred grove in southern Greece. That was Olympia during the Games. Tradition dates the first Olympics to 776 BC, and they were held every four years in honour of Zeus, king of the gods. The four-year gap, called an Olympiad, became so important that Greeks used it to count years. The sanctuary held temples, altars and treasuries, and the centrepiece was a huge gold and ivory statue of Zeus made by the sculptor Phidias, later counted among the Seven Wonders of the Ancient World. On the middle day of the festival, priests sacrificed a hundred oxen to Zeus, and the meat fed the crowds."],
    ["The sacred truce", "A truce let athletes and spectators travel safely to Olympia.", "Greece was not one country but hundreds of independent city-states, which were often at war with each other. To allow people to reach the Games, messengers travelled around the Greek world announcing a sacred truce, called the ekecheiria. During this period, athletes, trainers and spectators were supposed to be able to travel safely, and armies were not to enter the territory of Elis, the city-state that ran Olympia. The truce did not stop wars in general, and it was sometimes broken. In 420 BC, Sparta was banned from the Games and fined after being accused of violating it. Still, the idea of a pause in fighting for sport has inspired the modern Olympic movement, which promotes its own Olympic Truce."],
    ["Running the stadion", "The first and most prestigious event was a sprint of one stadium length.", "For the earliest Olympics, tradition says there was just one event: the stadion, a footrace of one length of the track, roughly 190 metres. The winner gave his name to that Olympiad, so being the fastest man in Greece made you famous for generations. Our word 'stadium' comes from this race. Over time more events were added: longer foot races, wrestling, boxing, the pentathlon, chariot racing and horse racing, and a race in which men ran wearing armour. The pentathlon combined five contests: running, wrestling, discus, javelin and long jump. Long jumpers swung stone or metal weights called halteres to carry themselves further. You can still walk through the stone tunnel into the ancient stadium at Olympia and stand on the starting line."],
    ["Naked, oiled and fierce", "Athletes competed naked, and combat sports were brutal.", "Male athletes competed naked, their bodies rubbed with olive oil. The Greeks saw the trained male body as something to be admired, and nudity in sport was part of their culture. The combat events were tough. Boxers wrapped their hands in leather straps rather than padded gloves, and bouts continued until one man gave up or could not go on. The most dangerous event was the pankration, a mix of boxing and wrestling with very few rules: biting and gouging the eyes were banned, but almost everything else was allowed, including strangleholds and bending fingers. Fighters sometimes died. Champions such as the wrestler Milo of Croton, said to have won at six Olympics, became legends whose stories were retold for centuries."],
    ["Women and the Games", "Married women were barred, but women still found ways to compete.", "According to the ancient writer Pausanias, married women were not allowed to watch the Games, although unmarried girls may have been. Women could not compete in the main athletic events. Yet there was a loophole. In chariot racing, the victory went to the owner of the horses, not the driver. A Spartan princess named Kyniska bred and trained horses, and her team won the four-horse chariot race, probably in 396 BC and again in 392 BC. She became the first woman to be named an Olympic victor, and a statue of her stood at Olympia. Separately, a festival called the Heraia, held in honour of the goddess Hera, included footraces for unmarried young women at Olympia itself."],
    ["Olive wreaths and big rewards", "The official prize was a wreath, but fame brought real riches.", "An Olympic winner received a crown of wild olive leaves cut from a sacred tree near the temple of Zeus. There were no silver or bronze medals; only winning counted. But the wreath was just the start. Victors returned home as heroes, and their cities might reward them with cash, free meals for life, statues and honoured seats at public events. Poets such as Pindar were paid to write victory odes. With such rewards on offer, cheating happened. Athletes caught bribing opponents were fined, and the money was used to make bronze statues of Zeus, called Zanes, which lined the path into the stadium. Their bases recorded the offenders' names, a permanent public shaming that visitors can still see today."],
    ["The end and the revival", "The ancient Games faded away and were reborn in 1896.", "The Olympics survived the Roman conquest of Greece, and Romans took part too. The emperor Nero entered a chariot race in AD 67, fell from his chariot and was still declared the winner. Over the following centuries, as Christianity became the official religion of the Roman Empire, old pagan festivals were suppressed. The Games are commonly said to have ended in the late 300s or 400s under emperors such as Theodosius I, although the exact date is debated. Olympia was later damaged by earthquakes and floods, and buried. In the 1800s, archaeologists uncovered it, and the French educator Pierre de Coubertin championed a modern revival. The first modern Olympics were held in Athens in 1896, and the marathon was a modern invention."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Which god were the ancient Olympics held to honour?", ["Apollo", "Poseidon", "Zeus", "Ares"], 2, "The Games were a religious festival in honour of Zeus."],
    [2, "What was the stadion?", ["A wrestling match", "A footrace of one stadium length", "A chariot race", "A javelin contest"], 1, "The stadion was a sprint of about one track length and gave us the word 'stadium'."],
    [3, "What was the pankration?", ["A relay race", "A combat sport mixing boxing and wrestling", "A music competition", "A swimming race"], 1, "The pankration combined boxing and wrestling with very few rules."],
    [4, "How did Kyniska become an Olympic victor?", ["She disguised herself as a man", "She won the women's footrace", "She owned the winning chariot team", "She was given an honorary title"], 2, "Victory in chariot racing went to the owner, so Kyniska's horses won her the title."],
    [5, "What were the Zanes at Olympia?", ["Bronze statues of Zeus paid for by cheaters' fines", "Gold medals", "Judges' robes", "Temples to Hera"], 0, "Fines from cheats paid for the Zanes, which named the offenders on their bases."]
  ]);

WB.L("byzantine-empire", "ancient", "intermediate", "Byzantium: the Roman Empire that didn't fall", "When Rome fell in the West, its eastern half carried on for almost another thousand years. From its glittering capital Constantinople, the Byzantine Empire kept Roman law, Greek learning and Christian art alive.",
  [
    ["Rome's other half", "The eastern Roman Empire survived until 1453.", "Most of us learn that the Roman Empire fell in AD 476, when the last emperor in the West was deposed. But that is only half the story. The empire had been divided into western and eastern halves, and the eastern half kept going for nearly another thousand years. Its people called themselves Romans, and their emperors saw themselves as the direct heirs of Augustus. The name 'Byzantine' was popularised by European scholars long after the empire ended, taken from Byzantium, the old Greek town on whose site the capital was built. Over the centuries the empire shrank and grew again, and Greek replaced Latin as its main language, but its rulers never stopped thinking of themselves as Roman."],
    ["The city on two continents", "Constantinople's location made it rich and hard to conquer.", "In AD 330, the emperor Constantine dedicated a new capital on the site of Byzantium, on the narrow strait of the Bosporus that separates Europe from Asia. It became known as Constantinople, the city of Constantine, and today it is Istanbul. The site was brilliant. It controlled the sea route between the Black Sea and the Mediterranean and stood on land routes between continents, so trade and taxes flowed in. It was surrounded on three sides by water, and in the 400s the emperor Theodosius II built massive land walls across the fourth side, with a moat and multiple layers of defences. For around 800 years those walls held off countless attackers, making Constantinople one of the best-protected cities on Earth."],
    ["Justinian and Theodora", "An ambitious emperor and empress reshaped the empire in the 500s.", "Justinian, who ruled from 527 to 565, wanted to restore Rome's old glory. His generals reconquered North Africa, Italy and part of Spain, although holding them proved costly. His wife Theodora had been an actress, a low-status job in Roman society, yet she became one of the most powerful women in Byzantine history. During the Nika riots of 532, when rioting crowds burned much of the capital and Justinian considered fleeing, the historian Procopius records that Theodora persuaded him to stay and fight. The revolt was crushed brutally. Justinian's reign was also struck by a terrible plague from 541, one of the first recorded outbreaks of the disease, which killed a large share of the population."],
    ["Hagia Sophia and the law", "Justinian left behind a great church and a great legal code.", "After the riots, Justinian rebuilt the city's main church, Hagia Sophia, meaning Holy Wisdom. Completed in 537 in only about five years, it was crowned by a vast dome that seems to float on a ring of windows. For about a thousand years it was the largest cathedral in the world. It later became a mosque, then a museum, and in 2020 a mosque again. Justinian's other great legacy was legal. He ordered experts to gather and organise centuries of Roman laws and legal opinions into one collection, later known as the Corpus Juris Civilis, or Body of Civil Law. Rediscovered in Western Europe in the Middle Ages, it shaped the legal systems of many countries that still use civil law today."],
    ["Greek fire and gold coins", "Secret weapons and a strong currency helped Byzantium survive.", "In the late 600s, Arab fleets besieged Constantinople. The Byzantines fought back with a terrifying new weapon now called Greek fire, a burning liquid sprayed through tubes from ships that kept burning even on water. Its recipe was a closely guarded state secret, and it was guarded so well that we still do not know exactly what was in it. Money mattered as much as weapons. The Byzantine gold coin, the solidus, kept a stable gold content for several centuries, and it was trusted by merchants from Europe to Asia. The empire also used diplomacy cleverly, paying neighbours, arranging marriages and setting enemies against each other. Many of its survivals owed as much to gold and cunning as to soldiers."],
    ["Faith, art and alphabets", "Byzantine Christianity spread a culture across Eastern Europe.", "Religion was at the heart of Byzantine life. Debates about theology could spark riots, and arguments over religious images, known as iconoclasm, divided the empire in the 700s and 800s. Over centuries, differences grew between the Church in Constantinople and the Church in Rome, and in 1054 a formal split, now called the Great Schism, separated what became the Orthodox and Catholic churches. In the 800s, two brothers from Thessaloniki, Cyril and Methodius, were sent as missionaries to the Slavs. They created an alphabet for Slavic languages, and their followers developed the Cyrillic script, named in Cyril's honour. Today it is used for Russian, Ukrainian, Bulgarian, Serbian and other languages, a living legacy of Byzantium across Eastern Europe."],
    ["The long fall", "Crusaders and Ottomans finally brought the empire down.", "The empire's greatest wound came from fellow Christians. In 1204, the knights of the Fourth Crusade, who were supposed to be heading for the Holy Land, attacked and sacked Constantinople instead, looting its treasures. The Byzantines recaptured their city in 1261, but the empire never recovered its strength. Meanwhile, the Ottoman Turks expanded steadily, until by the 1400s the empire was little more than the capital itself. In 1453, Sultan Mehmed II besieged Constantinople with a huge army and powerful cannons, and on 29 May the city fell. The last emperor, Constantine XI, died in the fighting. Greek scholars who fled to Italy brought manuscripts with them, helping to fuel the Renaissance and ensuring ancient Greek learning reached the modern world."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What did the Byzantines call themselves?", ["Greeks", "Byzantines", "Romans", "Constantinians"], 2, "They saw themselves as Romans, the continuation of the Roman Empire."],
    [1, "What made Constantinople so hard to capture?", ["It was built on a mountain", "Water on three sides and massive land walls", "A huge standing army of mercenaries", "Its location deep inland"], 1, "The sea protected three sides and the Theodosian Walls guarded the land side."],
    [3, "What was the Corpus Juris Civilis?", ["A collection of Roman laws", "A Byzantine war manual", "A book of church hymns", "A list of emperors"], 0, "Justinian's legal collection later shaped civil law systems across Europe."],
    [4, "What was unusual about Greek fire?", ["It could only be used on land", "It was made of gold", "It kept burning even on water", "It was invented by the Vikings"], 2, "Greek fire was sprayed from ships and kept burning on the sea's surface."],
    [6, "Who sacked Constantinople in 1204?", ["The Ottoman Turks", "The Mongols", "Knights of the Fourth Crusade", "Arab fleets"], 2, "The Fourth Crusade diverted to Constantinople and looted the city."]
  ]);

WB.L("persian-empire", "ancient", "intermediate", "Persia: the first superpower", "Long before Rome, a Persian king built an empire stretching from Egypt to India. The Achaemenid Persians ran it with roads, couriers, coins and a surprising degree of tolerance.",
  [
    ["Cyrus the Great", "Cyrus built a vast empire in a single lifetime.", "Around 550 BC, a ruler named Cyrus, from a region called Persis in what is now south-western Iran, overthrew the powerful Median kingdom that had dominated his people. Within about twenty years he had conquered the wealthy kingdom of Lydia in Asia Minor, the Greek cities on its coast, and in 539 BC the great city of Babylon. His dynasty is known as the Achaemenids, after a legendary ancestor. Cyrus's son Cambyses added Egypt, and his later successor Darius pushed the borders east into the Indus Valley and west into Europe. At its height the Persian Empire covered more land than any empire before it, and historians estimate it ruled a very large share of the world's population at the time."],
    ["The Cyrus Cylinder", "Cyrus presented himself as a liberator, not a destroyer.", "In 1879, archaeologists in Babylon found a clay cylinder roughly the size and shape of a rugby ball, covered in cuneiform writing. Now in the British Museum, the Cyrus Cylinder records how Cyrus took Babylon and claims that he restored temples and allowed displaced peoples to return to their homelands with the statues of their gods. The Hebrew Bible separately praises Cyrus for allowing Jewish exiles in Babylon to return to Jerusalem and rebuild their temple. Some modern writers have called the cylinder the first charter of human rights, but historians warn that this goes too far. It is royal propaganda in a long Mesopotamian tradition. Still, it reflects a real Persian policy: rule by working with local customs, not crushing them."],
    ["Darius organises an empire", "Darius divided the empire into provinces run by governors.", "Darius I, who ruled from 522 to 486 BC, was the great organiser. He divided the empire into provinces called satrapies, each run by a governor known as a satrap. Satraps collected taxes and kept order, but the king also sent inspectors, whom the Greeks nicknamed 'the eyes and ears of the king', to check on them. Darius introduced a gold coin called the daric and standardised weights and measures. Imperial officials often wrote in Aramaic, which became a common administrative language across the Near East. Darius also recorded his rise to power on a huge rock carving at Behistun in western Iran, in three languages. Centuries later, that inscription helped scholars such as Henry Rawlinson crack the code of cuneiform writing."],
    ["The Royal Road", "A highway and relay system carried messages across the empire.", "To hold such a vast realm together, the Persians needed fast communication. The most famous route was the Royal Road, which ran roughly 2,500 kilometres from Sardis, near the Aegean coast, to Susa, one of the royal capitals. Along it were stations where official messengers could find fresh horses, food and rest. The Greek historian Herodotus was impressed: he wrote that nothing could stop these couriers from completing their stages as fast as possible, not snow, rain, heat or darkness. A version of his words is carved on the main post office building in New York City. Messages that might take a traveller on foot three months could reportedly be relayed by the royal couriers in about a week or so."],
    ["Persepolis", "The ceremonial capital displayed the empire's diversity.", "Darius began building a magnificent palace complex at Persepolis, in the Persian homeland. Its great audience hall, the Apadana, was supported by tall stone columns topped with carved animals such as bulls. The most striking carvings show long processions of delegates from across the empire bringing gifts to the king: Babylonians, Egyptians, Indians, Ethiopians, Lydians and many more, each shown in their own clothing and carrying local goods. Rather than scenes of slaughter, the art projects an image of peaceful order, with many peoples united under one ruler. Clay tablets found at the site record payments of food and wages to workers, including skilled craftspeople, and show that some women received rations and were paid as workers too."],
    ["Faith and fire", "Zoroastrianism shaped Persian ideas about truth and order.", "Many Persian kings honoured Ahura Mazda, the wise lord at the centre of Zoroastrianism, one of the world's oldest surviving religions. Its prophet, Zarathustra, known to the Greeks as Zoroaster, taught about a struggle between truth and order on one side and the lie and chaos on the other. Darius's inscriptions repeatedly attack 'the Lie' and present the king as its enemy. Fire was treated as a sacred symbol of purity, a tradition still important in Zoroastrian fire temples today. Exactly how the kings' beliefs related to later Zoroastrian practice is debated by scholars. Some ideas from this tradition, such as a final judgement and a struggle between good and evil, may have influenced later religions, though that question is also debated."],
    ["Wars with Greece and the end", "Persia's Greek wars and Alexander's conquest shaped its reputation.", "Much of what Westerners learned about Persia came from its enemies. Darius and his son Xerxes invaded Greece in 490 and 480 BC and were famously defeated at battles such as Marathon and Salamis. Greek writers portrayed the Persians as luxurious and tyrannical, a picture that coloured European views for centuries. In reality, Persia remained a great power for another 150 years. Its end came when Alexander the Great invaded, defeated Darius III in a series of battles, and took the empire. In 330 BC Persepolis was burned, whether by accident, revenge or celebration is still argued. Yet Alexander adopted many Persian ways of ruling, and later Iranian empires, such as the Parthians and Sasanians, would rise again."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Which great city did Cyrus capture in 539 BC?", ["Athens", "Babylon", "Memphis", "Jerusalem"], 1, "Cyrus took Babylon in 539 BC, a major step in building his empire."],
    [1, "How do historians generally view the claim that the Cyrus Cylinder was the first human rights charter?", ["As fully accurate", "As an exaggeration of royal propaganda", "As a modern forgery", "As a Greek invention"], 1, "Historians see it as royal propaganda, though it reflects a real policy of tolerance."],
    [2, "What were satraps?", ["Persian priests", "Elite soldiers", "Provincial governors", "Royal couriers"], 2, "Satraps governed provinces called satrapies on behalf of the king."],
    [3, "Roughly how long was the Royal Road from Sardis to Susa?", ["About 250 kilometres", "About 800 kilometres", "About 2,500 kilometres", "About 10,000 kilometres"], 2, "The road stretched roughly 2,500 kilometres across the empire."],
    [4, "What do the carvings at Persepolis mainly show?", ["Battles against the Greeks", "Delegates from many lands bringing gifts", "Hunting scenes", "The life of Zoroaster"], 1, "The processions show peoples from across the empire bringing tribute to the king."]
  ]);

WB.L("bronze-age-collapse", "ancient", "advanced", "The Bronze Age collapse", "Around 1200 BC, a connected world of kingdoms around the eastern Mediterranean fell apart within a few generations. Palaces burned, writing vanished in places, and historians still debate why.",
  [
    ["A globalised ancient world", "Late Bronze Age kingdoms were linked by trade and diplomacy.", "In the 1300s BC, the eastern Mediterranean was surprisingly connected. Egypt, the Hittite kingdom in Anatolia, Babylon, Assyria, the Mycenaean Greeks and wealthy trading cities such as Ugarit on the Syrian coast exchanged goods, gifts and letters. Diplomatic letters found at Amarna in Egypt show kings addressing one another as 'brother' and haggling over gold, horses and royal marriages. The letters were mostly written in Akkadian cuneiform, the shared diplomatic language of the age. Palaces at the centre of these kingdoms ran complex economies, recording grain, livestock and workers on clay tablets. It was, in some ways, an early globalised system, and like modern globalised systems, it depended on many parts working together at once."],
    ["Why bronze needed the world", "Bronze required tin, which had to travel huge distances.", "Bronze is made by mixing copper with a small amount of tin, roughly one part tin to nine parts copper. Copper was fairly available, with Cyprus a major source. Tin was much rarer, and in this region it often had to be brought from far away, possibly from Central Asia among other sources. That made the whole Bronze Age economy dependent on long trade routes. A shipwreck found off Uluburun in southern Turkey, dating to the late 1300s BC, shows how rich this trade was. It carried about ten tonnes of copper ingots and about a tonne of tin, along with glass, ivory, ebony, gold jewellery and goods from at least half a dozen cultures. Bronze, and power, rested on these fragile networks."],
    ["The great unravelling", "Within a few generations, many great centres were destroyed.", "In the decades around 1200 BC, things began to break. Many Mycenaean palaces in Greece, including Pylos, were destroyed by fire. The Hittite capital Hattusa was abandoned and burned, and the Hittite kingdom, once a rival to Egypt, disappeared. Ugarit was destroyed and never rebuilt. Cities in Cyprus and the Levant were damaged or abandoned. Not everywhere fell at once, and the process probably took several decades, but by about 1150 BC the old system had collapsed. Egypt survived, but weakened and shrunken. Assyria and Babylon endured in reduced form. In Greece, the palace-based writing system known as Linear B stopped being used, and people seem to have lost the ability to write for several centuries."],
    ["The Sea Peoples", "Mysterious raiders appear in Egyptian records of the time.", "On the walls of his mortuary temple at Medinet Habu, the pharaoh Ramesses III recorded a great victory over invaders from the sea, usually dated to his eighth year, around 1177 BC. The inscriptions claim that foreign lands were swept away and that no land could stand before these attackers, whom modern historians call the Sea Peoples. The carvings show ships and warriors in distinctive headdresses, along with families travelling in ox carts, suggesting migration rather than only raiding. Some of these groups are named, and one, the Peleset, is often linked to the Philistines of the Bible. For a long time the Sea Peoples were blamed for the whole collapse, but many historians now think they were as much a symptom as a cause."],
    ["Drought, quakes and famine", "Natural disasters likely added to the pressure.", "Scientists studying ancient pollen, lake sediments and cave formations have found evidence of a period of drier conditions in the eastern Mediterranean around this time. A drier climate means poorer harvests, and texts from the period mention grain shortages. Letters suggest the Hittites asked Egypt for grain shipments, and one letter to Ugarit urgently requests ships to carry food. Some researchers have also argued that a series of earthquakes struck the region over several decades, damaging cities. Each of these pressures on its own might have been survivable. Together, they would have strained palace economies that relied on surplus food to feed workers and soldiers, making it harder for rulers to keep control and easier for unrest and migration to spread."],
    ["A systems collapse", "The best explanation may be many problems hitting at once.", "Today, many historians, including Eric Cline in his popular book '1177 BC', argue that no single cause explains the collapse. Instead they describe a 'perfect storm' in which drought, famine, earthquakes, migrations, raids, internal revolts and broken trade routes all interacted. In a tightly connected system, a shock in one place spreads to others. If tin stopped arriving, bronze weapons and tools could not be made. If grain shipments failed, cities went hungry. If palaces fell, the scribes, craftsmen and traders who depended on them scattered. This idea, sometimes called systems collapse, draws on research into how complex societies and networks fail. It also explains why the collapse was so widespread, even though no single invader could have conquered so many kingdoms."],
    ["What came after", "From the ruins came iron, new peoples and the alphabet.", "The centuries that followed are sometimes called a dark age, partly because written records become scarce. Population fell and many settlements shrank. Yet the collapse also cleared space for new things. Iron, which could be made from widely available ore rather than imported tin, gradually came into wide use, giving the Iron Age its name. Smaller states emerged, including the Phoenician cities, the kingdoms of Israel and Judah, and new Aramaean states. Phoenician traders spread a simple alphabet of about two dozen letters, much easier to learn than cuneiform or hieroglyphs. The Greeks later adapted it, and from it descend the Latin letters you are reading now. The story is a reminder that connected systems bring wealth, but can also fall together."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What did the Amarna letters reveal about Late Bronze Age kings?", ["They rarely communicated", "They called each other 'brother' and negotiated gifts and marriages", "They all spoke Greek", "They were ruled by one emperor"], 1, "The letters show a lively diplomatic network among rulers of great kingdoms."],
    [1, "Why did the Bronze Age economy depend on long trade routes?", ["Copper was found only in Egypt", "Tin needed for bronze was rare and had to travel far", "Horses were imported from Europe", "Bronze could only be made at sea"], 1, "Bronze required tin, which was scarce in the region and often came from far away."],
    [2, "Which writing system fell out of use in Greece after the collapse?", ["Linear B", "Cuneiform", "Hieroglyphs", "The Greek alphabet"], 0, "Linear B, used by Mycenaean palaces, disappeared with them."],
    [5, "What does 'systems collapse' suggest about the end of the Bronze Age?", ["One invader conquered everyone", "Many linked problems combined and spread through a connected network", "A single volcano caused it", "It never actually happened"], 1, "The theory holds that interacting shocks brought down an interdependent system."],
    [6, "What lasting innovation was spread by Phoenician traders after the collapse?", ["Bronze", "Cuneiform", "A simple alphabet", "Paper"], 2, "The Phoenician alphabet was later adapted by the Greeks and led to Latin letters."]
  ]);

WB.L("antikythera-mechanism", "ancient", "advanced", "The Antikythera mechanism: an ancient computer", "Divers searching for sponges found a lump of corroded bronze in a Roman-era shipwreck. More than a century later, scientists realised it was a geared machine that modelled the heavens.",
  [
    ["Treasure from the deep", "Sponge divers stumbled on an ancient shipwreck in 1900.", "In 1900, a group of Greek sponge divers sheltering from a storm near the small island of Antikythera, between Crete and the Greek mainland, dived and found an ancient shipwreck. Over the next year, working with only basic diving gear, they and the Greek government recovered bronze and marble statues, glassware, coins and pottery. The ship had sunk in the first century BC, probably carrying luxury goods towards Italy. Among the finds were some unremarkable lumps of corroded bronze and wood. In 1902, the archaeologist Valerios Stais noticed a gear wheel embedded in one of them. Few people could believe that the ancient Greeks had built anything so intricate, and for decades the object remained a puzzle sitting in a museum in Athens."],
    ["Gears hidden in rock", "Scientists spent a century working out what the device was.", "The mechanism survives in 82 fragments, with the largest about the size of a hand. Inside are at least 30 interlocking bronze gears, some with teeth only around a millimetre or two apart. In the 1950s, the British physicist and historian Derek de Solla Price began studying it seriously, and with X-ray images in the 1970s he proposed that it was a calendar computer. Then, in 2005, a team called the Antikythera Mechanism Research Project used high-resolution X-ray CT scanning and advanced surface imaging. These revealed hidden gears and thousands of tiny inscribed letters, many unseen for two thousand years. The inscriptions turned out to be a kind of user manual, describing what the dials showed and how to read them."],
    ["Turning a handle to see the sky", "Turning a crank moved dials showing the sun, moon and calendar.", "The mechanism was housed in a wooden box roughly the size of a shoebox, with dials on the front and back. A user turned a handle on the side, moving the date forward or backward, and the gears carried the motion to pointers on the dials. The front showed the zodiac and a calendar of 365 days, with pointers for the positions of the sun and moon. A small ball, half pale and half dark, rotated to show the phase of the moon. Researchers believe the front also displayed the planets known to the ancient Greeks: Mercury, Venus, Mars, Jupiter and Saturn. In effect, it was a hand-powered model of the cosmos as seen from Earth."],
    ["Predicting eclipses", "The back dials tracked long cycles used to forecast eclipses.", "The back of the device held two large spiral dials. One followed the Metonic cycle, the discovery that 19 solar years almost exactly match 235 lunar months, which helped keep lunar and solar calendars in step. The other tracked the Saros cycle of 223 lunar months, a period after which similar eclipses tend to recur. Small inscriptions on the spiral marked months when solar or lunar eclipses could be expected, and some even noted details such as the likely time of day. These cycles were known from centuries of Babylonian astronomical records. The Greeks combined that knowledge with their own geometric models of the heavens and turned the results into gears, so a user could predict eclipses years into the future."],
    ["A clever pin and slot", "An ingenious gear captured the moon's changing speed.", "One of the mechanism's most impressive features deals with a subtle problem. The moon does not move across the sky at a steady speed. Because its orbit is an ellipse, it moves faster when closer to Earth and slower when further away. Greek astronomers such as Hipparchus, in the second century BC, developed geometric models to describe this. The mechanism's designers reproduced the effect with two gears mounted slightly off-centre from one another, linked by a pin in one gear that slid in a slot in the other. As they turned, the moon's pointer sped up and slowed down. This pin-and-slot arrangement shows a sophisticated understanding of both astronomy and mechanical engineering, packed into a few centimetres of bronze."],
    ["Games, dates and who made it", "A dial even tracked the Olympic Games and other athletic festivals.", "One small dial on the back tracked a four-year cycle of major Panhellenic games, including the Olympics. It is a reminder that this was a practical as well as a scientific device: in a world of many local calendars, festivals helped people mark time. Who made it, and when, is still debated. Estimates for its construction range across the second and early first centuries BC. The month names on the calendar have been linked to Corinthian colonies, possibly in north-western Greece or Sicily. Some have connected it to the tradition of Archimedes, who lived in Syracuse and was said to have built a sphere showing the motions of the heavens, but there is no proof he was involved."],
    ["A lost technology", "Nothing as complex is known for more than a thousand years.", "The most startling thing about the mechanism is that it is unique. No other geared device of comparable complexity survives from the ancient world, and nothing similar is known until astronomical clocks appeared in medieval Europe more than a thousand years later. Ancient writers, including Cicero, mention devices that showed the movements of the heavens, so it was probably not the only one. Bronze was valuable and often melted down for reuse, which may explain why others did not survive. In 2021, a team at University College London published a new reconstruction of the front display, showing how the planets might have been geared. Today the fragments are on display at the National Archaeological Museum in Athens, still challenging what we assume about ancient technology."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Who found the Antikythera shipwreck in 1900?", ["Archaeologists from Athens", "Greek sponge divers", "British naval officers", "Fishermen from Crete"], 1, "Sponge divers sheltering from a storm discovered the wreck."],
    [1, "What did the 2005 CT scans reveal?", ["The device was a modern fake", "Hidden gears and thousands of tiny inscribed letters", "That it was made of gold", "The name of its owner"], 1, "Advanced imaging showed hidden gears and inscriptions that acted like a user manual."],
    [3, "What was the Saros cycle used for?", ["Predicting eclipses", "Measuring tides", "Counting harvests", "Timing Olympic races"], 0, "The Saros cycle of 223 lunar months helps predict when similar eclipses recur."],
    [4, "What did the pin-and-slot gear arrangement model?", ["The changing length of days", "The moon's varying speed across the sky", "The rising of the sun", "The movement of the tides"], 1, "Off-centre gears linked by a pin made the moon pointer speed up and slow down."],
    [6, "Why might so few devices like this have survived?", ["They were banned by law", "Bronze was valuable and often melted down", "They were made of paper", "They were all lost in one fire"], 1, "Bronze was commonly recycled, so similar devices were probably melted down."]
  ]);
